{"author":{"id":"7674679dac9170669f752d932f8b5955b1d55717359e13b1c1e687003c7bb99f"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791483070,"nonce":"vzNw9M/+WN3zaYEPP6NwmH1Hp/w=","title":"A level is all keyed rows or none, and a row with no id names its type","message":"Two judgement calls of the query-rows implementation (0204a6ae, c9aa6237), reversed on review 2026-10-08.\n\n1. A level never mixes keyed and plain rows: the rows of one level under one parent (the roots across their groups, a listed children array, a layer's children of one row) are all keyed (key != id, or no id) or none. A mixed level is refused naming a key and an id. The mixed-scope drag handling goes: rank fills skipping a keyed issue, a plain drop updating a kept order.\n2. A row with no id names its type in fields.type, a type the schema knows, else it is refused naming its key. The fallback drawing a field as the kind every type agrees on goes.\n\nDesign: doc/design/query-rows.md R2, R5, Settled questions 4.","files":null,"fields":{"area":["tui"],"parent":"d1c547a29a529c3d66f5d279c99ad39a1884342e78a721483e5e6a13f1d8c11a","priority":"medium","status":"in-progress","type":"task"}}]}